anti-abortion activists demonstrating  outside mexico's supreme court on  tuesday they're protesting a landmark  decision by mexico's high court  declaring that the criminalization of  abortion in the state of cuahuilla is  unconstitutional  the unanimous ruling sets a precedent  for the decriminalization of abortion  nationwide  ladies and gentlemen ministers today is  a historic day for the rights of all  mexican women and pregnant people  from this moment on it will not be  possible to prosecute any woman who has  an abortion in the cases that this  constitutional court has considered  valid  [Music]  women's groups and advocates of abortion  rights are celebrating what they call a  watershed moment  the court has spoken abortion should not  be punitive it should not be  criminalized  this resolution will give legal recourse  to women who today are imprisoned to  regain their freedom  experts warn that while the wheels are  in motion for the decriminalization of  abortion across mexico  it will continue to inflame divisions in  a country with deep conservative  catholic traditions  with this decision by the supreme court  mexico becomes the most populous country  in latin america to move toward  decriminalization of abortion mexico is  also one of the most catholic countries  in the world and conservative rights  groups have vowed to continue to push  back against the court's decision on  abortion rights  the move in favor of abortion in mexico  comes at a time when across the border  u.s states are restricting women's  access to terminations particularly  texas many in mexico believe tuesday's  court ruling will eventually allow u.s  women to turn to mexico for safe and  legal abortions that women will no  longer be prosecuted  that that would include all all women  not only mexican women but mexican  territory  but as it does not mean that services  have to be provided as such then we  won't be expecting necessarily that  women from texas would find more access  to services in mexico than they would in  their own country but this is what we  would be aspiring to in the near future  mexico's president not an outspoken  proponent of abortion rights stressed  that the decision was the courts to make  and promise not to interfere  manuel rapalo al jazeera mexico city
 

